Best primary sources
1. Al-Qushayri — Epistle on Sufism

This is the most directly useful after the “watcher watching you” idea. It has an actual chapter on muraqaba, translated as “Awareness [of God],” and defines it through the Hadith of Ihsan: worship as though you see God, knowing that He sees you. It also defines muraqaba as awareness of God’s presence together with scrutiny of one’s actions and thoughts.

Use in Imaginarium: attention becomes relational. You are not an isolated observer watching contents; you are a watched being learning to participate in the Watcher’s field.

2. Al-Ghazali — Ihya’ ‘Ulum al-Din, especially Books 21, 36, 38, 39

For your purposes, do not read the whole Ihya first. Go straight to:

Book 21 — Marvels of the Heart
Book 36 — Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment
Book 38 — Vigilance and Self-Examination
Book 39 — Meditation / Contemplation

Book 38 is explicitly Kitab al-muraqaba wa’l-muhasaba, the book of vigilance and self-examination, and its structure is basically a full inner-practice engine: intention, vigilance, self-examination, renewed striving, and self-censure.

Use in Imaginarium: the heart as the rendering root; action as the branch. This is perfect for “re-rendering” because Ghazali treats perception, intention, attention, desire, and action as one system.

3. Al-Muhasibi — Risala al-Mustarshidin / Treatise for the Seekers of Guidance

This is likely the most practical Muhasibi entry point in English. Zaid Shakir’s translation is described as a guide to Islamic spirituality and moral psychology, presenting major ideas for spiritual development.

Use in Imaginarium: the earliest “inner audit” model: the soul becomes legible by being watched, examined, corrected, and returned.

4. Ibn Arabi — Meccan Revelations, selections

This is more useful than Fusus for your immediate project. James Morris’s Ibn Arabi Society materials specifically introduce The Meccan Revelations and related themes like spiritual practice, dreams, body of light, inspiration, and discernment.

Use in Imaginarium: cosmological rendering. The world is not just “matter plus symbols”; it is self-disclosure through levels of manifestation.

5. Ibn Arabi — barzakh / khayal selections

The standalone essay “Divine Imagination and the Intermediate World: Ibn ‘Arabi on the Barzakh” is extremely aligned. It explains Ibn Arabi’s imagination as the intermediate world, not mere fantasy, and says later Islamic philosophers, poets, artists, and critics used these ideas to understand creative and visionary activity.

The killer idea: imagination is the domain where realities become form. It gives form even to what intellect cannot form; it is “light” through which things are disclosed.

Use in Imaginarium: this is basically your “rendering layer” in classical Islamic metaphysics.

Best academic books from your list
1. Henry Corbin — Creative Imagination in the Sufism of Ibn Arabi

Essential. For Imaginarium, this is probably the single most important secondary source in the whole list. The JSTOR page summarizes the book around imagination as a magical intermediary between thought and being, and includes chapters such as “Creation as Theophany” and “Theophanic Imagination and Creativity of the Heart.”

Use: imaginal world, creative imagination, angelic mediation, mundus imaginalis, theophany.

2. William Chittick — The Sufi Path of Knowledge

Also essential, but more sober/technical than Corbin. It explains Ibn Arabi’s theology, ontology, epistemology, hermeneutics, and soteriology, and is explicitly subtitled around Ibn Arabi’s “metaphysics of imagination.”

Use: conceptual discipline. Corbin gives the mythic fire; Chittick gives the architecture.

3. Sachiko Murata — The Tao of Islam

Very good for Imaginarium, especially if you want polarity, complementarity, cosmology, masculine/feminine principles, and God-world-human relational structure. The book is a sourcebook on relationships between God and world, world and human being, and human being and God, using polarity in a way comparable to yin/yang.

Use: polarity engine, cosmological complementarity, symbolic structure.

4. Ahmet Karamustafa — Sufism: The Formative Period

This is the best historical corrective. It covers the ninth to twelfth centuries and uses primary sources to build a unified account of how Sufism developed.

Use: prevents the project from becoming “vibe Sufism.” Use it to place Muhasibi, Junayd, Bayazid, Hallaj, Qushayri, Sarraj, etc.

5. Annemarie Schimmel — Mystical Dimensions of Islam

Good broad literary/historical map. UNC Press publishes the 2011 edition with a foreword by Carl Ernst.

Use: breadth, poetry, saints, devotional texture, Persianate atmosphere.

6. Carl Ernst — Sufism: An Introduction to the Mystical Tradition of Islam

Best clean intro. Shambhala describes it as covering Sufi philosophy, practice, history, its relation to Islam, and encounters with fundamentalism/secularism.

Use: orientation before deeper reading.

Lower priority from your list

Claude Addas — Quest for the Red Sulphur
Good biography of Ibn Arabi, but less directly useful than Corbin/Chittick unless you want the lived arc of Ibn Arabi.

Knysh — Islamic Mysticism: A Short History
Good survey, but for Imaginarium I would use Karamustafa + Ernst first.

Trimingham — The Sufi Orders in Islam
Useful for tariqa/order history, less useful for your metaphysics/practice layer.

Julian Baldick — Mystical Islam
Fine intro, but not as central as Ernst, Schimmel, or Karamustafa. NYU Press lists it as an introduction to Sufism with a later afterword reflecting on scholarship.

Llewellyn Vaughan-Lee — Sufism: The Transformation of the Heart
Useful devotionally, but I would not classify it with the academic books. Read later for heart-language, not as a foundation.

Best standalone essays / papers to grab
1. Henry Corbin — “Mundus Imaginalis, or the Imaginary and the Imaginal”

Absolutely central. This is the essay version of the distinction you need: imaginal ≠ imaginary. Academia has copies/listings of it.

Why it matters: this gives Imaginarium its anti-reductionist language. The imaginal is not private fantasy; it is a mode of real disclosure.

2. James W. Morris — “Divine Imagination and the Intermediate World: Ibn ‘Arabi on the Barzakh”

This is one of the best short bridges into Ibn Arabi. It directly covers barzakh, imagination, dreams, vision, and symbolic perception.

Why it matters: it gives you the classical source for “rendering as intermediate manifestation.”

3. Angela Voss — “Becoming an Angel: the mundus imaginalis of Henry Corbin and the Platonic path of self-knowledge”

Academia has this. It links Corbin, the imaginal, Platonism, alchemy, angelic self-knowledge, and spiritual transformation.

Why it matters: this is perfect for your Ficino/Corbin bridge.

4. “Techniques of Practicing Muraqaba by Sufis in Malay Archipelago”

Not as metaphysically deep, but useful for practical muraqaba. The abstract frames muraqaba as a Sufi meditation method for attaining ma‘rifa, gnosis of God.

Why it matters: practical technique layer.

5. “The Ihsan Hadith as the Spiritual Foundation of Muraqabah”

Useful because it locks muraqaba to the Hadith of Ihsan: worship as seeing / being seen. The search result describes it as comparing muraqabah with contemporary mindfulness while grounding it in Sahih Muslim’s Hadith of Jibril.

Why it matters: this is exactly the “watching because you are watched” seam.

6. “Ibn Arabi and the Imaginal World…”

SSRN has a recent essay on Ibn Arabi’s ‘alam al-khayal as an intermediate realm where divine truths become symbolically perceivable.

Why it matters: potentially useful for modern comparisons, though I’d treat it as secondary/experimental, not foundational.

